{
  "id": "Compound::DB01234 - Disease::DOID:7148",
  "verbalization": "Dexamethasone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Rheumatoid Arthritis through a shared mechanism involving glucocorticoid receptor activation. The evidence suggests that Dexamethasone, through its glucocorticoid receptor-mediated pathways, contributes to the development of joint instability, polymorphonuclear leukocytosis, and loss of skin markings, all of which are associated with inflammatory and immunological processes. These effects are mediated by the therapeutic glucocorticoid class, which is known to modulate immune responses and reduce inflammation. The presence of Betamethasone, a closely related glucocorticoid, as a causal link further supports the notion that Dexamethasone exerts its effects through a common biological pathway. The integration of these findings implies that Dexamethasone may be repurposed for the treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis, given its ability to modulate inflammatory processes. The mechanism is supported by the shared ontological relationship with 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, reinforcing the biological relevance of glucocorticoid action in this context.",
